1.8.0: polavision - history's answer to the instant movie

"Instant cameras do not shoot movies" had exactly one historical
exception, and now it is camera #14: polavision, Polaroid's 1977
additive-color instant movie system. Sealed cassette (takes no film,
like its ancestor autochrome), dim muted low-contrast image with
colored grain - the additive filter screen's signature - Super-8-gauge
gate, and native motion character: camera weave and player flicker.
Motion-native in superjuice with no counterfactual note; refused
nothing, because this one actually happened.

The sealed-process message now mentions cassettes; the frozen matrix
grows to 35 cells; camera-count assertions rise to 14; PRESET_SOURCES
records the inspired-by honesty (no measured Polavision
characterization is known to exist).
